WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.440
I was 8 and my
brother was about 10.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.710
We really wanted to go
camping without any adults.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:12.720
My parents agreed as
long as we trained.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.640
We were living in
the Arctic, so it

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.960
was cold temperatures and
storms and blizzards and bears.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.910
But we wanted them to succeed in
their little mini first outing.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.760
We wanted to raise the kids,
giving them the confidence

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.200
to do impossible things.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:39.100
You learn to do
things yourself

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.220
because we need
to be able to deal

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:45.470
with the worst case scenarios.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:49.430
The reality is if it's
blowing 100 kilometers an hour

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:53.540
and it's a storm, we're out
in the middle of nowhere.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.010
Nobody's coming out to get you.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:00.980
My parents knew that as well.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:04.129
There's no rescue operation
if something bad happens.

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:06.800
There's no Hollywood
grand finale.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:07.760
We'll be frozen.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:08.660
We'll be dead.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.040
We'll be eaten by a polar bear.

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.340
Go.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:26.110
So this ice behind us is
quite impressive to look at,

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:28.480
but it's not very
impressive when

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.770
we're traveling through it.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.590
We weren't that far out
when I remember my brother

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.090
had this, like, shock.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:44.840
What do you see from up there?

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.440
Mostly more ice.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:52.190
We wanted to cross
the Boothia Strait.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:56.310
Looking out at it, it seemed
like a really bad idea.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:00.170
But we knew if we pushed
across, which was the quickest

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:02.270
way, there's the
risk that somebody

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:06.900
would fall through the water
and just disappear out there.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.450
So we're going to camp the
night and see if it solidifies.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.880
A detour would add
about 500 kilometers

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.180
to our total trip, which
would put us in jeopardy

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:18.980
of running out of snow and ice.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:38.320
So we started the
day in good hopes

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.070
that we could get
to the other side.

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:43.550
See, that's a fox.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:44.800
There's something out there.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.350
Birds don't make
that [BLEEP] sound.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:50.140
Which would be neat
to see an Arctic fox,

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:55.160
but at the same time, foxes
tend to follow polar bears.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.670
Polar Bears are a huge risk
anywhere in the Arctic,

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:02.717
especially around
open water 'cause

00:03:02.717 --> 00:03:03.925
that's where they hunt seals.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:15.200
Our biggest fear was having a
polar bear encounter in camp.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.540
We had a bear fence--

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:21.500
basically, a tripwire, which
would hopefully wake us up.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.770
They're so quiet, they
can sneak up on anything.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:32.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

